  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nHigh pressure remains in place today, currently centered over PA, \nwhile plenty of high clouds are filtering in from low pressure over \nthe Dakotas. The combination of return southerly flow off the \naformentioned high pressure along with 500-mb height increasing \nslightly will bring high temperatures to the low-and-mid 80s, while \ndew points creep into the 70s; especially away from the lake. \n\nIncreasing clouds overnight as low pressure wraps up over interior \nOntario and a trailing cold front approaches. By sunrise, the chance \nof rain increases as well, through preceding mid-level dry air may \nerode some of this Wednesday morning rainfall. We remain under a \nmarginal risk for severe thunderstorms Wednesday afternoon. The \npicture has not changed much as bubbling afternoon heat and \ninstability along and south of the US 24 corridor will accommodate \nthunderstorms, yet upper-level flow remains well removed to the \nnorth and improving shear lags behind the best instability. \nCertainly not a widespread severe weather event and thunderstorms \nwill likely struggle to gain elevation due to incredibly small \nlapse rates. \n\nAdditionally, for Wednesday, depending on the extend of \nmorning cloud cover along the incoming front, there ought to be a \nfairly notable temperature gradient from south to north where \nsouthern counties may creep toward 90s, yet northern areas will be \nconfined to the low-80s. The existing temperature forecast is on the \nhot-side of guidance and will be highly dependent on aformentioned \ncloud cover. \n\nSome showers may linger into early Thursday morning, but drying out \notherwise and not as hot for the afternoon. A progressive upper-air \npattern takes shape for the remainder of the week, suppressing an \nupper-level ridge to our south, and paving the way for additional \nrain chances (20-30%) later in the coming weekend.",
